Module 1:GIS Fundamentals
-GIS stands for Geographic Information System.
-It's a system used to collect, store, analyze, and display information about places on Earth.
-QGIS is simply the software we'll use.
-A map is many layers stacked together.

Two main types of GIS data

✅ Vector data
Represents features using points, lines, and polygons.
Point → Hospitals, schools, bus stops.
Line → Roads, rivers, power lines.
Polygon → Counties, parks, lakes, buildings.
✅ Raster data
Represents the world as a grid of pixels.
Examples:
Satellite images
Aerial photographs
Elevation maps
Temperature maps


Geographic Features
We learned that everything on a map is called a feature.Example school,river building...

Types of Geographic Features(Geometry)
the three geometry types:
Point-is a single location like school,hospital,atm,tree,bus stop
Line-is something that has length like roads,rivers,railway,power line
Polygon-is Something that has an area like Country,Forest,Lake,Farm,Building

One important thing to remember
-Whether something is a point, line, or polygon depends on the level of detail.

For example:
Imagine you're making a map of Africa.
Nairobi could be represented as a point because it's too small to draw as an area on a continent-wide map.

Now imagine you're making a map of Nairobi County.
Nairobi County would be a polygon because now you're showing its boundaries.So the same real-world object can sometimes be represented differently depending on the map's scale.

✅ Feature – Any object on a map (road, river, school, building, forest, etc.)
✅ Spatial data –(Asks Where the feature is) The location and shape of a feature.(point,line,polygon)  Maps = Spatial Data
✅ Attribute data –(Asks What the feature is) Information stored about the feature.(name,type ...)  Tables = Attribute Data

Difference Between Spatial Data and Attribute Data
Always remember this:
Spatial data answers "Where?"
-The hospital is located here.
-The road runs from Nairobi to Thika.
-Karura Forest is represented by a polygon.
Attribute data answers "What?"
-Name,Area,Population,Number of beds,Tree species,Length of a road

How to Identify Spatial vs Attribute Data
Whenever you're confused, ask yourself:
-Can I draw it on a blank map?
Imagine I give you a blank sheet of paper.
Information:
Karura Forest has 1,000 tree species.
Can you draw 1,000 tree species?
❌ No.
You can write it in a table, but you can't draw it.
So it's an attribute.



Module 2: Getting Started with QGIS
Understanding the QGIS Interface
Menu Bar – Access to all QGIS functions.
Toolbar – Quick-access buttons for common tools.
Locator Bar – Search for tools, layers, commands, and more.
Browser Panel – Browse your files and data sources.
Layers Panel – Manage the layers in your project.
Map Canvas – The area where your map is displayed.
Status Bar – Shows coordinates, scale, CRS, and other project information.


Your First QGIS Project
Every time they start a new mapping task:
Create a project folder.
Save the QGIS project immediately.
Keep all related data inside that folder.
This keeps everything organized.


Adding Your First Layer
-Layer – A collection of related geographic data displayed on a map.Each layer is independent.
-Think of the Layers Panel as a stack of transparent sheets.
-Layer Order-is The order in which layers are stacked. Layers at the top are drawn above layers below them.order makes the map clear and easy to understand
-Layer order matters because layers on top can hide layers below.
-Symbology is how a layer looks on the map.
-OpenStreetMap (OSM) is a free, editable map of the world created by people all over the world.
-Adding OpenStreetMap gives us a basemap.A basemap is A map used as the background for displaying other geographic information its like the background of your project. It helps you understand where things are.
-Examples of basemaps:
OpenStreetMap (OSM)
Satellite imagery
Topographic maps
Street maps


Adding Your First Basemap (OpenStreetMap)
Step 1

In the Browser Panel, click the small arrow (▶) next to XYZ Tiles and It should expand.
You should see something like:XYZ Tiles
   			 OpenStreetMap
Simply double-click "OpenStreetMap."
Wait a few seconds.
Your white map canvas should start loading a map of the world.
You've just added your first GIS layer.



Module 3: Working with Vector Data
What is Vector Data?
A vector layer is a collection of geographic features of the same type.

Examples:

Roads → Line layer
Hospitals → Point layer
Counties → Polygon layer

Each layer also has an attribute table, which stores information about the features.

Attribute Table
Every feature has two parts:

Geometry – The shape (point, line, or polygon).
Attributes – Information about the feature.

Example:

Hospital Name			Type		Beds
Kenyatta National Hospital	Referral	1800
Mbagathi Hospital		County		350

The hospital appears as a point on the map, while the table stores its details.